1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,167
Dawn, October 13th, 1951.

2
00:00:03,187 --> 00:00:05,182
A needle-sharp peak in the mountains

3
00:00:05,202 --> 00:00:07,412
north of Yanggu, South Korea.

4
00:00:07,549 --> 00:00:09,521
French soldiers were clawing their way

5
00:00:09,541 --> 00:00:12,030
up the slope toward that final summit.

6
00:00:12,358 --> 00:00:13,916
It took one full month to

7
00:00:13,936 --> 00:00:15,567
take this single ridge.

8
00:00:15,704 --> 00:00:17,949
Casualties in the U.S. 2nd Infantry

9
00:00:17,969 --> 00:00:19,667
Division and its attached units

10
00:00:19,687 --> 00:00:22,382
passed three thousand seven hundred.

11
00:00:22,579 --> 00:00:24,831
Three peaks strung together like the edge of

12
00:00:24,851 --> 00:00:30,761
a blade. Hill 894, Hill 931, and Hill 851.

13
00:00:30,926 --> 00:00:32,375
War correspondents gave it

14
00:00:32,395 --> 00:00:34,739
a name: Heartbreak Ridge.

15
00:00:34,945 --> 00:00:38,424
In Korean, the name became 'Danjang-ui

16
00:00:38,444 --> 00:00:41,610
Neungseon.' The Ridge of the Severed Bowels,

17
00:00:41,630 --> 00:00:44,196
a grief so deep it cuts you in two.

18
00:00:44,385 --> 00:00:46,042
And here is the strange part.

19
00:00:46,103 --> 00:00:48,496
In one month, American guns fired

20
00:00:48,516 --> 00:00:49,714
more than half a million

21
00:00:49,734 --> 00:00:52,059
recorded rounds onto this ridge.

22
00:00:52,103 --> 00:00:54,530
And yet for over two weeks, the infantry

23
00:00:54,550 --> 00:00:57,147
could not stand on its highest peak.

24
00:00:57,197 --> 00:00:59,651
What was really happening up on that ridge?

25
00:00:59,829 --> 00:01:01,488
In the end, what broke this

26
00:01:01,508 --> 00:01:03,537
ridge was not more shells.

27
00:01:03,557 --> 00:01:05,621
It was a single road through the valley,

28
00:01:05,641 --> 00:01:08,332
carved out with shovels and explosives.

29
00:01:08,529 --> 00:01:10,573
The Battle of Heartbreak Ridge.

30
00:01:10,642 --> 00:01:12,626
This is the story of thirty days of

31
00:01:12,646 --> 00:01:15,846
mountain hell, and of Operation Touchdown,

32
00:01:15,866 --> 00:01:18,581
the plan that finally ended it.

33
00:01:18,765 --> 00:01:21,844
July 1951. At Kaesong,

34
00:01:21,864 --> 00:01:24,154
armistice talks finally began.

35
00:01:24,214 --> 00:01:25,878
But outside the conference room,

36
00:01:25,898 --> 00:01:28,974
the war did not stop for a single day.

37
00:01:29,031 --> 00:01:31,367
On August 23rd, the Communists broke

38
00:01:31,387 --> 00:01:33,416
off the talks, claiming UN

39
00:01:33,436 --> 00:01:36,272
aircraft had bombed the neutral zone.

40
00:01:36,475 --> 00:01:37,961
With the talks frozen, the UN

41
00:01:37,981 --> 00:01:39,986
command's logic became simple.

42
00:01:40,086 --> 00:01:41,967
Whatever could not be won at the table

43
00:01:41,987 --> 00:01:44,342
would be won on the battlefield.

44
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:46,435
General James Van Fleet,

45
00:01:46,455 --> 00:01:48,836
commander of the U.S. Eighth Army.

46
00:01:48,932 --> 00:01:50,505
Instead of a grand offensive,

47
00:01:50,525 --> 00:01:52,486
he ordered limited attacks to seize

48
00:01:52,506 --> 00:01:55,558
key ground, one hill at a time.

49
00:01:55,666 --> 00:01:57,786
The idea was to squeeze the enemy at the

50
00:01:57,806 --> 00:02:00,242
front, and push him back to the table.

51
00:02:00,430 --> 00:02:02,907
The stage was the rugged mountain country

52
00:02:02,927 --> 00:02:05,844
north of Yanggu, in Gangwon Province.

53
00:02:05,932 --> 00:02:08,098
To the east yawned a huge bowl-shaped

54
00:02:08,118 --> 00:02:11,387
basin the Americans called the Punchbowl.

55
00:02:11,449 --> 00:02:13,113
This sector belonged to U.S.

56
00:02:13,133 --> 00:02:15,265
X Corps, and at its center stood

57
00:02:15,285 --> 00:02:17,518
the U.S. 2nd Infantry Division.

58
00:02:17,706 --> 00:02:19,958
On September 5th, after three weeks

59
00:02:19,978 --> 00:02:21,712
of savage fighting, the 2nd

60
00:02:21,732 --> 00:02:24,295
Division finally took Bloody Ridge.

61
00:02:24,418 --> 00:02:25,935
The very ridge we covered in an

62
00:02:25,955 --> 00:02:27,987
earlier episode of this channel.

63
00:02:28,159 --> 00:02:30,014
Bloody Ridge alone had cost more

64
00:02:30,034 --> 00:02:31,550
than two thousand eight hundred

65
00:02:31,570 --> 00:02:34,270
UN and South Korean casualties.

66
00:02:34,368 --> 00:02:36,431
On the crest, the North Koreans left

67
00:02:36,451 --> 00:02:38,973
behind more than five hundred dead.

68
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:41,704
But the North Koreans had not collapsed.

69
00:02:41,749 --> 00:02:43,413
They had simply pulled back a few

70
00:02:43,433 --> 00:02:45,986
kilometers north, to a ridge that

71
00:02:46,006 --> 00:02:48,096
was higher and steeper still.

72
00:02:48,116 --> 00:02:49,860
And that ridge had already

73
00:02:49,880 --> 00:02:51,858
been turned into a fortress.

74
00:02:52,048 --> 00:02:54,404
For the men standing on Bloody Ridge,

75
00:02:54,424 --> 00:02:58,022
another great wall now rose before their eyes.

76
00:02:58,120 --> 00:03:00,969
A rocky ridgeline, thick with forest,

77
00:03:00,989 --> 00:03:02,925
stretching beyond sight.

78
00:03:03,024 --> 00:03:04,575
Higher than Bloody Ridge.

79
00:03:04,595 --> 00:03:07,011
Longer. And steeper.

80
00:03:07,184 --> 00:03:09,322
A massive spine of mountains running

81
00:03:09,342 --> 00:03:11,984
some eleven kilometers north to south.

82
00:03:12,049 --> 00:03:13,816
With the Mundung-ni Valley to the

83
00:03:13,836 --> 00:03:15,933
west and the Sat'ae-ri Valley to the

84
00:03:15,953 --> 00:03:19,255
east, it rose like a knife's edge.

85
00:03:19,419 --> 00:03:21,485
Leave it in enemy hands, and he

86
00:03:21,505 --> 00:03:23,260
could keep watching Bloody Ridge and

87
00:03:23,280 --> 00:03:25,623
every UN position to the south.

88
00:03:25,789 --> 00:03:27,065
The attack order came from

89
00:03:27,085 --> 00:03:29,318
Brigadier General Thomas de Shazo,

90
00:03:29,338 --> 00:03:31,526
then acting division commander.

91
00:03:31,605 --> 00:03:33,052
He believed a single regiment

92
00:03:33,072 --> 00:03:34,297
could take the ridge.

93
00:03:34,486 --> 00:03:37,044
The thinking was that an enemy just driven

94
00:03:37,064 --> 00:03:39,935
off Bloody Ridge must already be exhausted.

95
00:03:40,104 --> 00:03:41,530
Only the division's artillery

96
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:43,328
commander predicted the enemy would

97
00:03:43,348 --> 00:03:45,660
fight like hell for this ridge.

98
00:03:45,865 --> 00:03:46,862
Underestimating the

99
00:03:46,882 --> 00:03:48,614
enemy's strength and will,

100
00:03:48,634 --> 00:03:51,382
was the first step into thirty days of hell.

101
00:03:51,585 --> 00:03:54,057
Now let's take a closer look at this ridge.

102
00:03:54,212 --> 00:03:57,254
Heartbreak Ridge had three major peaks.

103
00:03:57,443 --> 00:03:59,862
At the southern end, Hill 894,

104
00:03:59,882 --> 00:04:01,887
looking down on Bloody Ridge.

105
00:04:01,942 --> 00:04:04,848
The highest, Hill 931. And to the

106
00:04:04,868 --> 00:04:08,179
north, the needle-like Hill 851.

107
00:04:08,361 --> 00:04:11,087
From Hill 894 to Hill 931 was

108
00:04:11,107 --> 00:04:13,215
about one point two kilometers.

109
00:04:13,270 --> 00:04:16,289
From Hill 931 to Hill 851,

110
00:04:16,309 --> 00:04:19,039
another one point nine kilometers.

111
00:04:19,247 --> 00:04:20,656
The crest connecting the peaks

112
00:04:20,676 --> 00:04:22,773
was so narrow that only a few

113
00:04:22,793 --> 00:04:25,368
men could stand side by side.

114
00:04:25,458 --> 00:04:27,805
On either side, the slopes plunged

115
00:04:27,825 --> 00:04:30,473
steeply all the way to the valley floor.

116
00:04:30,678 --> 00:04:32,697
Holding the ridge was the North Korean

117
00:04:32,717 --> 00:04:35,610
6th Division, under General Hong Nim.

118
00:04:35,733 --> 00:04:37,785
To the west, around Mundung-ni,

119
00:04:37,805 --> 00:04:40,528
troops of the North Korean 12th Division

120
00:04:40,548 --> 00:04:42,507
held the high ground as well.

121
00:04:42,538 --> 00:04:45,306
And behind Hill 851 to the north,

122
00:04:45,326 --> 00:04:48,406
units of the 13th Division stood in support.

123
00:04:48,586 --> 00:04:50,055
The North Koreans had turned the

124
00:04:50,075 --> 00:04:52,536
entire ridge into a fortress.

125
00:04:52,625 --> 00:04:54,631
Bunkers roofed with layer upon

126
00:04:54,651 --> 00:04:56,384
layer of logs and earth,

127
00:04:56,404 --> 00:04:59,058
linked by communication trenches.

128
00:04:59,167 --> 00:05:01,835
Knock out one bunker, and the ones beside

129
00:05:01,855 --> 00:05:04,011
it and behind it would cover each other.

130
00:05:04,199 --> 00:05:05,666
On top of that, the ridge

131
00:05:05,686 --> 00:05:07,624
was covered in dense forest.

132
00:05:07,751 --> 00:05:09,575
Trees and undergrowth hid the

133
00:05:09,595 --> 00:05:12,216
positions so well that aerial photos

134
00:05:12,236 --> 00:05:14,200
could barely find the bunkers.

135
00:05:14,379 --> 00:05:16,147
Even deadlier were the mortars

136
00:05:16,167 --> 00:05:17,934
hidden beyond the crest.

137
00:05:18,059 --> 00:05:19,917
From positions no one could see,

138
00:05:19,937 --> 00:05:21,898
rounds rained down on the

139
00:05:21,918 --> 00:05:24,366
valleys and slopes without pause.

140
00:05:24,556 --> 00:05:25,726
The new division commander

141
00:05:25,746 --> 00:05:27,615
later gave his assessment.

142
00:05:27,752 --> 00:05:29,246
About eighty-five percent of the

143
00:05:29,266 --> 00:05:31,374
division's casualties in this period,

144
00:05:31,394 --> 00:05:34,229
he said, came from enemy mortars.

145
00:05:34,430 --> 00:05:35,906
The attackers had to start from the

146
00:05:35,926 --> 00:05:37,635
valley floor and climb these

147
00:05:37,655 --> 00:05:40,003
slopes with nothing to hide behind.

148
00:05:40,118 --> 00:05:41,727
From above, every man coming

149
00:05:41,747 --> 00:05:43,791
up the hill was in plain view.

150
00:05:43,979 --> 00:05:45,040
Bunkers, forest,

151
00:05:45,060 --> 00:05:46,917
and mortars no one could see.

152
00:05:47,048 --> 00:05:47,972
For the attackers,

153
00:05:47,992 --> 00:05:49,720
there was nowhere to hide.

154
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:51,967
And at dawn on September 13th,

155
00:05:51,987 --> 00:05:54,952
1951, the attack began.

156
00:05:55,149 --> 00:05:57,468
At five thirty in the morning, a thirty-minute

157
00:05:57,488 --> 00:06:00,177
preparatory barrage pounded the ridge.

158
00:06:00,285 --> 00:06:02,485
Then the 3rd and 2nd Battalions of

159
00:06:02,505 --> 00:06:05,113
the U.S. 23rd Infantry crossed the

160
00:06:05,133 --> 00:06:07,341
Sat'ae-ri Valley toward the ridge.

161
00:06:07,530 --> 00:06:09,281
But the troops were stopped cold at

162
00:06:09,301 --> 00:06:11,887
the very first spur below the ridge.

163
00:06:12,003 --> 00:06:13,576
Machine guns opened up from

164
00:06:13,596 --> 00:06:15,158
bunkers hidden in the woods,

165
00:06:15,178 --> 00:06:17,857
and mortar rounds came pouring down.

166
00:06:18,057 --> 00:06:20,141
The next day, the 2nd Battalion

167
00:06:20,161 --> 00:06:22,452
of the 9th Infantry moved against

168
00:06:22,472 --> 00:06:25,308
Hill 894 at the southern end.

169
00:06:25,408 --> 00:06:26,970
And on September 15th,

170
00:06:26,990 --> 00:06:30,497
they finally stood on top of Hill 894.

171
00:06:30,575 --> 00:06:32,296
It gave them a single foothold on

172
00:06:32,316 --> 00:06:34,011
the southern tip of the ridge.

173
00:06:34,197 --> 00:06:36,151
But the North Koreans immediately launched

174
00:06:36,171 --> 00:06:38,643
two straight days of counterattacks.

175
00:06:38,812 --> 00:06:41,103
In those two days of holding the hill,

176
00:06:41,123 --> 00:06:44,406
the 9th Infantry lost more than two hundred men.

177
00:06:44,602 --> 00:06:46,995
On September 16th, the North Koreans

178
00:06:47,015 --> 00:06:49,237
pulled out a worn-down unit and sent

179
00:06:49,257 --> 00:06:51,342
a fresh regiment onto the ridge.

180
00:06:51,462 --> 00:06:53,400
Every time the Americans attacked,

181
00:06:53,420 --> 00:06:56,256
the enemy filled the gaps with fresh troops.

182
00:06:56,447 --> 00:06:58,134
What remained was the highest

183
00:06:58,154 --> 00:07:01,371
objective of all: Hill 931.

184
00:07:01,548 --> 00:07:03,827
September 16th. The 21st

185
00:07:03,847 --> 00:07:06,786
through the 23rd. And the 26th.

186
00:07:06,906 --> 00:07:09,811
Again and again, the 23rd Infantry

187
00:07:09,831 --> 00:07:12,366
threw itself at Hill 931.

188
00:07:12,557 --> 00:07:14,636
Whenever they crawled close to the summit,

189
00:07:14,656 --> 00:07:17,595
grenades came tumbling down like rain.

190
00:07:17,760 --> 00:07:19,686
Even when they got a toehold on one

191
00:07:19,706 --> 00:07:21,871
side of the peak, the enemy swarmed

192
00:07:21,891 --> 00:07:24,022
back at night and drove them off.

193
00:07:24,161 --> 00:07:27,516
Up the same slope, and up again. And on that

194
00:07:27,536 --> 00:07:30,370
slope, the fallen were left where they lay.

195
00:07:30,542 --> 00:07:32,394
The night of September 17th.

196
00:07:32,414 --> 00:07:35,205
Private First Class Herbert Pililaau,

197
00:07:35,225 --> 00:07:38,220
Company C, 23rd Infantry.

198
00:07:38,271 --> 00:07:40,611
A twenty-two-year-old from Hawaii.

199
00:07:40,801 --> 00:07:42,910
When his platoon was ordered to pull back,

200
00:07:42,930 --> 00:07:45,813
he volunteered to stay behind and cover them.

201
00:07:45,896 --> 00:07:47,618
He fired his automatic rifle,

202
00:07:47,638 --> 00:07:50,361
threw every grenade he had, and fought at the

203
00:07:50,381 --> 00:07:53,149
end with his bare fists until he fell.

204
00:07:53,259 --> 00:07:54,946
Around the spot he defended,

205
00:07:54,966 --> 00:07:58,076
more than forty enemy dead were found.

206
00:07:58,246 --> 00:08:00,149
Meanwhile, Colonel John Lynch of the

207
00:08:00,169 --> 00:08:03,336
9th Infantry proposed another way.

208
00:08:03,456 --> 00:08:04,505
Not straight up the front,

209
00:08:04,525 --> 00:08:06,452
but strike other hills to the west

210
00:08:06,472 --> 00:08:08,477
and shake the enemy's flank.

211
00:08:08,574 --> 00:08:10,693
It was turned down at first, but when

212
00:08:10,713 --> 00:08:12,480
a new division commander arrived on

213
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:15,401
September 20th, the plan was approved.

214
00:08:15,729 --> 00:08:18,858
On September 26th, Colonel James Adams,

215
00:08:18,878 --> 00:08:21,055
commander of the 23rd Infantry,

216
00:08:21,075 --> 00:08:23,081
reported to his superiors.

217
00:08:23,197 --> 00:08:24,269
Any further attack,

218
00:08:24,289 --> 00:08:26,230
he said, would be suicide.

219
00:08:26,413 --> 00:08:29,065
By then, the 23rd Infantry alone had taken

220
00:08:29,085 --> 00:08:31,603
more than nine hundred fifty casualties,

221
00:08:31,623 --> 00:08:33,583
and the division's losses were climbing

222
00:08:33,603 --> 00:08:36,306
past one thousand six hundred seventy.

223
00:08:36,511 --> 00:08:38,735
Finally, on September 27th,

224
00:08:38,755 --> 00:08:41,170
the frontal assaults were called off.

225
00:08:41,276 --> 00:08:43,419
After two weeks of spilled blood,

226
00:08:43,439 --> 00:08:46,788
Hill 931 was still in enemy hands.

227
00:08:46,964 --> 00:08:49,040
Around this time, the war correspondents

228
00:08:49,060 --> 00:08:51,578
covering the front gave the ridge its name.

229
00:08:51,742 --> 00:08:53,169
Heartbreak Ridge.

230
00:08:53,358 --> 00:08:55,541
A place where you climbed and fell,

231
00:08:55,561 --> 00:08:58,306
where every peak you took was taken back.

232
00:08:58,385 --> 00:09:00,527
For the men who fought there, it truly

233
00:09:00,547 --> 00:09:03,070
was the ridge that broke your heart.

234
00:09:03,259 --> 00:09:05,500
In the middle of this hell, one unit

235
00:09:05,520 --> 00:09:08,129
fought alongside the 23rd Infantry.

236
00:09:08,156 --> 00:09:09,821
The French Battalion of the United

237
00:09:09,841 --> 00:09:12,873
Nations Command, all the way from France.

238
00:09:13,062 --> 00:09:15,347
Its commander was Ralph Monclar.

239
00:09:15,475 --> 00:09:18,389
Born Raoul Magrin-Vernerey, he was a

240
00:09:18,409 --> 00:09:20,313
legend of the French Foreign Legion who

241
00:09:20,333 --> 00:09:22,623
had fought through both World Wars.

242
00:09:22,682 --> 00:09:25,086
Born in 1892, he was nearly

243
00:09:25,106 --> 00:09:26,971
sixty when he came to Korea.

244
00:09:27,161 --> 00:09:28,639
But here is the thing: he

245
00:09:28,659 --> 00:09:30,266
was already a general.

246
00:09:30,295 --> 00:09:32,427
He had led the Foreign Legion at Narvik

247
00:09:32,447 --> 00:09:35,307
in Norway in 1940, and fought the

248
00:09:35,327 --> 00:09:38,151
war as a general of the Free French.

249
00:09:38,268 --> 00:09:39,807
Yet to lead a single battalion

250
00:09:39,827 --> 00:09:41,867
himself, he took a voluntary

251
00:09:41,887 --> 00:09:43,744
demotion to lieutenant colonel.

252
00:09:43,825 --> 00:09:45,626
Because a battalion was a

253
00:09:45,646 --> 00:09:47,478
lieutenant colonel's command.

254
00:09:47,658 --> 00:09:49,858
Made up entirely of volunteers,

255
00:09:49,878 --> 00:09:51,314
the French Battalion fought

256
00:09:51,334 --> 00:09:54,489
attached to the U.S. 23rd Infantry.

257
00:09:54,527 --> 00:09:57,387
That February at Chipyong-ni, surrounded

258
00:09:57,407 --> 00:10:00,244
by Chinese forces alongside the 23rd,

259
00:10:00,264 --> 00:10:02,897
they had held their ground to the end.

260
00:10:03,089 --> 00:10:04,430
At Heartbreak Ridge too,

261
00:10:04,450 --> 00:10:06,217
the French went into the assaults on

262
00:10:06,237 --> 00:10:09,460
Hill 931 with the 23rd Infantry.

263
00:10:09,495 --> 00:10:11,376
In night attacks and close combat,

264
00:10:11,396 --> 00:10:13,970
it is said, they never gave ground.

265
00:10:14,060 --> 00:10:17,067
Bunker by bunker, with grenades and bayonets.

266
00:10:17,087 --> 00:10:19,494
It was the most brutal kind of fighting.

267
00:10:19,698 --> 00:10:22,130
At Chipyong-ni, according to one story,

268
00:10:22,150 --> 00:10:23,599
they had even charged with

269
00:10:23,619 --> 00:10:25,976
bayonets to the sound of whistles.

270
00:10:26,161 --> 00:10:28,936
On September 26th, in an attack near

271
00:10:28,956 --> 00:10:31,827
Pia-ri, one French sergeant charged

272
00:10:31,847 --> 00:10:33,989
ahead into the enemy positions,

273
00:10:34,044 --> 00:10:35,743
and was later awarded the U.S.

274
00:10:35,763 --> 00:10:37,671
Distinguished Service Cross.

275
00:10:37,877 --> 00:10:39,354
At Heartbreak Ridge, the French

276
00:10:39,374 --> 00:10:41,426
Battalion lost sixty killed

277
00:10:41,446 --> 00:10:43,758
and about two hundred wounded.

278
00:10:43,856 --> 00:10:45,783
Together, the 23rd Infantry and

279
00:10:45,803 --> 00:10:47,730
the French Battalion bore almost

280
00:10:47,750 --> 00:10:50,076
half of the division's casualties.

281
00:10:50,265 --> 00:10:52,215
And it would be this French Battalion that

282
00:10:52,235 --> 00:10:54,627
wrote the final chapter on this ridge.

283
00:10:54,749 --> 00:10:56,551
But first, the division had

284
00:10:56,571 --> 00:10:58,723
another problem it had to solve.

285
00:10:58,921 --> 00:11:00,590
The attacks had not failed only

286
00:11:00,610 --> 00:11:02,570
because of what was on the ridge.

287
00:11:02,688 --> 00:11:04,114
The real problem lay in the

288
00:11:04,134 --> 00:11:05,848
narrow valleys below it.

289
00:11:06,031 --> 00:11:07,803
The only ways to the ridge

290
00:11:07,823 --> 00:11:09,783
were a few narrow valleys.

291
00:11:09,871 --> 00:11:12,675
The Pia-ri Valley, which became the supply

292
00:11:12,695 --> 00:11:15,568
route, was choked with trucks and jeeps.

293
00:11:15,760 --> 00:11:17,563
And that valley lay entirely within

294
00:11:17,583 --> 00:11:20,487
range of enemy artillery and mortars.

295
00:11:20,578 --> 00:11:21,980
Wherever a vehicle stopped,

296
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:24,000
shells began to fall.

297
00:11:24,201 --> 00:11:26,043
On mountain trails no truck could

298
00:11:26,063 --> 00:11:27,932
climb, ammunition and food

299
00:11:27,952 --> 00:11:30,139
were carried by Korean laborers.

300
00:11:30,263 --> 00:11:32,462
With wooden A-frames on their backs,

301
00:11:32,482 --> 00:11:33,521
they climbed the steep

302
00:11:33,541 --> 00:11:36,229
slopes again and again every day.

303
00:11:36,376 --> 00:11:38,326
Unarmed, they were the nameless

304
00:11:38,346 --> 00:11:40,788
supply line that held up the front.

305
00:11:40,970 --> 00:11:43,099
But when the enemy came close, many of

306
00:11:43,119 --> 00:11:45,432
them dropped their loads and scattered.

307
00:11:45,561 --> 00:11:47,625
It is hard to blame unarmed men

308
00:11:47,645 --> 00:11:50,481
climbing slopes under falling shells.

309
00:11:50,577 --> 00:11:52,822
In the end, American infantrymen

310
00:11:52,842 --> 00:11:54,927
had to become porters themselves,

311
00:11:54,947 --> 00:11:57,222
hauling ammunition up the hill.

312
00:11:57,419 --> 00:12:00,085
Even harder was bringing the wounded down.

313
00:12:00,232 --> 00:12:02,295
Carrying a single litter from the ridge to

314
00:12:02,315 --> 00:12:05,076
the valley could take as long as ten hours.

315
00:12:05,253 --> 00:12:07,626
On the steep slopes, a litter needed not

316
00:12:07,646 --> 00:12:10,722
four men but six or seven to move it at all.

317
00:12:10,742 --> 00:12:13,988
And that path, too, lay under enemy mortars.

318
00:12:14,133 --> 00:12:16,128
Some days, there were more men carrying

319
00:12:16,148 --> 00:12:18,165
and hauling than men fighting.

320
00:12:18,352 --> 00:12:20,199
Even shells ran short.

321
00:12:20,288 --> 00:12:22,477
Two thousand five hundred rounds a day were

322
00:12:22,497 --> 00:12:24,890
flown in from Pusan for four days,

323
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:28,148
and another twenty-five thousand came by rail.

324
00:12:28,341 --> 00:12:30,768
A high ridge, narrow valleys,

325
00:12:30,788 --> 00:12:32,816
and broken supply lines.

326
00:12:32,871 --> 00:12:35,549
Attacks that only rammed the front had run

327
00:12:35,569 --> 00:12:38,784
into a dead end right here, in these valleys.

328
00:12:38,981 --> 00:12:41,378
By then, the division had a new commander,

329
00:12:41,398 --> 00:12:43,653
Major General Robert Young.

330
00:12:43,769 --> 00:12:45,365
He summed up the attacks so far

331
00:12:45,385 --> 00:12:48,153
in a single word: a fiasco.

332
00:12:48,339 --> 00:12:49,509
And he pulled out a completely

333
00:12:49,529 --> 00:12:50,761
different solution.

334
00:12:50,816 --> 00:12:54,141
Not more shells, but a road for tanks.

335
00:12:54,330 --> 00:12:56,591
The operation was called Touchdown.

336
00:12:56,699 --> 00:12:58,272
In American football, it is the

337
00:12:58,292 --> 00:13:00,047
moment you drive all the way

338
00:13:00,067 --> 00:13:02,380
into the end zone and score.

339
00:13:02,470 --> 00:13:04,579
The name said it all: carry the

340
00:13:04,599 --> 00:13:06,650
ball deep into enemy ground,

341
00:13:06,670 --> 00:13:09,283
and send tanks around behind the enemy.

342
00:13:09,475 --> 00:13:11,287
First: never concentrate

343
00:13:11,307 --> 00:13:12,892
the attack on one spot.

344
00:13:13,014 --> 00:13:14,270
All three of the division's

345
00:13:14,290 --> 00:13:16,033
regiments would strike several points

346
00:13:16,053 --> 00:13:17,929
of the ridge at the same time.

347
00:13:18,117 --> 00:13:20,359
In fact, the first sign of this had

348
00:13:20,379 --> 00:13:22,942
already appeared in late September.

349
00:13:23,028 --> 00:13:25,990
The 9th Infantry had taken Hill 1024 to the

350
00:13:26,010 --> 00:13:28,654
west, and the South Korean 7th Division

351
00:13:28,674 --> 00:13:32,993
Hill 1142, prying open the ridge's flank.

352
00:13:33,177 --> 00:13:35,307
Second, and this was the key.

353
00:13:35,376 --> 00:13:37,064
Tanks would drive up the valley,

354
00:13:37,084 --> 00:13:38,768
deep behind the ridge.

355
00:13:38,964 --> 00:13:40,572
The problem was, there was

356
00:13:40,592 --> 00:13:42,256
no road for the tanks.

357
00:13:42,325 --> 00:13:44,218
The track up the Mundung-ni Valley

358
00:13:44,238 --> 00:13:46,995
was blocked by mines, huge boulders,

359
00:13:47,015 --> 00:13:49,423
and obstacles the enemy had built.

360
00:13:49,600 --> 00:13:50,962
The job fell to Lieutenant

361
00:13:50,982 --> 00:13:53,296
Colonel Robert Love and the U.S.

362
00:13:53,316 --> 00:13:55,647
2nd Engineer Combat Battalion.

363
00:13:55,829 --> 00:13:57,948
But as luck would have it, their bulldozers

364
00:13:57,968 --> 00:14:00,589
were broken down and under repair.

365
00:14:00,679 --> 00:14:02,605
So the engineers began cutting the road

366
00:14:02,625 --> 00:14:05,606
with nothing but shovels and explosives.

367
00:14:05,770 --> 00:14:08,128
Blasting away a single rock roadblock

368
00:14:08,148 --> 00:14:11,224
took about fifty kilograms of explosives.

369
00:14:11,313 --> 00:14:13,365
Where mines were buried, they linked

370
00:14:13,385 --> 00:14:15,949
charges together like a chain and set

371
00:14:15,969 --> 00:14:18,260
them off about every fifteen meters.

372
00:14:18,447 --> 00:14:19,949
Where there was no road at all,

373
00:14:19,969 --> 00:14:22,555
they simply ran it along the stream bed.

374
00:14:22,651 --> 00:14:25,226
And on October 10th, the tank road up the

375
00:14:25,246 --> 00:14:28,394
Mundung-ni Valley was finally finished.

376
00:14:28,578 --> 00:14:30,914
Meanwhile, in the Sat'ae-ri Valley to

377
00:14:30,934 --> 00:14:33,304
the east, Major Kenneth Sturman's tank

378
00:14:33,324 --> 00:14:36,217
task force shook the enemy with raids.

379
00:14:36,341 --> 00:14:37,676
It pulled the enemy's eyes

380
00:14:37,696 --> 00:14:39,207
in the wrong direction.

381
00:14:39,395 --> 00:14:41,141
On the evening of October 5th,

382
00:14:41,161 --> 00:14:44,044
Operation Touchdown opened behind strikes

383
00:14:44,064 --> 00:14:46,467
by Marine Corsair fighter-bombers.

384
00:14:46,693 --> 00:14:49,200
The 9th, 23rd and 38th Infantry,

385
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:52,716
and the French Battalion, all moved at once.

386
00:14:52,852 --> 00:14:55,119
Attached to the 38th Infantry,

387
00:14:55,139 --> 00:14:56,895
the Netherlands Battalion joined

388
00:14:56,915 --> 00:14:58,964
the attack on the eastern hills.

389
00:14:59,141 --> 00:15:01,607
At three in the morning on October 6th,

390
00:15:01,627 --> 00:15:03,490
the 23rd Infantry seized

391
00:15:03,510 --> 00:15:06,073
the southern part of Hill 931.

392
00:15:06,128 --> 00:15:08,623
And before noon that day, all of Hill

393
00:15:08,643 --> 00:15:11,685
931 was finally in American hands.

394
00:15:11,881 --> 00:15:14,113
The peak where so many had fallen for three

395
00:15:14,133 --> 00:15:16,707
weeks crumbled in a single night of attack.

396
00:15:16,781 --> 00:15:18,685
Because the enemy could no longer mass

397
00:15:18,705 --> 00:15:21,759
his firepower and reserves in one place.

398
00:15:21,955 --> 00:15:24,125
Around the same time, the surrounding

399
00:15:24,145 --> 00:15:26,265
hills fell one after another.

400
00:15:26,285 --> 00:15:29,384
The 38th Infantry took Hill 728 on October

401
00:15:29,404 --> 00:15:32,640
6th and Hill 636 on the 8th, and the

402
00:15:32,660 --> 00:15:36,430
9th Infantry took Hill 867 on the 7th.

403
00:15:36,503 --> 00:15:37,962
Now the enemy no longer knew

404
00:15:37,982 --> 00:15:39,885
which hole to plug first.

405
00:15:40,065 --> 00:15:42,878
October 10th. The Sherman tanks of Lieutenant

406
00:15:42,898 --> 00:15:45,895
Colonel John Woods's 72nd Tank Battalion

407
00:15:45,915 --> 00:15:48,931
roared north up the Mundung-ni Valley.

408
00:15:49,136 --> 00:15:50,684
The tanks cut straight through the

409
00:15:50,704 --> 00:15:53,368
enemy's supply routes behind the ridge.

410
00:15:53,489 --> 00:15:55,324
A Chinese unit coming forward to

411
00:15:55,344 --> 00:15:57,475
take over the positions was caught in

412
00:15:57,495 --> 00:15:59,707
the open valley under tank fire.

413
00:15:59,896 --> 00:16:01,157
The unit sent to relieve the

414
00:16:01,177 --> 00:16:04,219
North Koreans was the 204th Division

415
00:16:04,239 --> 00:16:06,666
of the Chinese 68th Army.

416
00:16:06,707 --> 00:16:08,280
The enemy on the ridge was

417
00:16:08,300 --> 00:16:11,168
now trapped, front and rear.

418
00:16:11,368 --> 00:16:13,686
On October 10th, the 9th Infantry

419
00:16:13,706 --> 00:16:16,645
took Hill 1005 with a bayonet charge,

420
00:16:16,665 --> 00:16:18,672
and the next day, the South Korean

421
00:16:18,692 --> 00:16:21,323
8th Division seized Hill 1050.

422
00:16:21,401 --> 00:16:23,715
One by one, the high peaks west of

423
00:16:23,735 --> 00:16:26,536
the ridge were falling into UN hands.

424
00:16:26,726 --> 00:16:28,631
At dawn on October 13th,

425
00:16:28,651 --> 00:16:32,148
the last peak left: Hill 851.

426
00:16:32,332 --> 00:16:33,848
Monclar's French Battalion

427
00:16:33,868 --> 00:16:35,300
stormed its summit.

428
00:16:35,494 --> 00:16:37,204
It was the moment a battle begun

429
00:16:37,224 --> 00:16:39,469
on September 13th came to an end,

430
00:16:39,489 --> 00:16:41,574
exactly one month later.

431
00:16:41,692 --> 00:16:44,620
Then on October 15th, the 38th Infantry

432
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:46,146
took its final objective,

433
00:16:46,166 --> 00:16:49,300
Hill 1220, and the operation was over.

434
00:16:49,499 --> 00:16:51,102
These are the numbers left by more

435
00:16:51,122 --> 00:16:53,025
than thirty days of fighting.

436
00:16:53,164 --> 00:16:56,001
U.S. 2nd Division and attached units: more

437
00:16:56,021 --> 00:16:58,777
than three thousand seven hundred casualties.

438
00:16:58,866 --> 00:17:00,929
Enemy losses, by the division's estimate,

439
00:17:00,949 --> 00:17:03,102
about twenty-five thousand.

440
00:17:03,297 --> 00:17:05,500
The 2nd Division's artillery fired more

441
00:17:05,520 --> 00:17:07,936
than four hundred thousand rounds from

442
00:17:07,956 --> 00:17:10,997
its 105-millimeter howitzers alone.

443
00:17:11,124 --> 00:17:12,801
The Air Force flew eight hundred

444
00:17:12,821 --> 00:17:14,405
forty-two sorties and dropped

445
00:17:14,425 --> 00:17:17,178
two hundred fifty tons of bombs.

446
00:17:17,374 --> 00:17:19,243
So why did the ridge hold out for more than

447
00:17:19,263 --> 00:17:22,648
three weeks against such enormous firepower?

448
00:17:22,668 --> 00:17:24,697
And what finally broke it open?

449
00:17:24,717 --> 00:17:26,508
Let's look at four reasons.

450
00:17:26,697 --> 00:17:29,624
First: underestimating the enemy.

451
00:17:29,818 --> 00:17:31,551
Believing the enemy was worn out

452
00:17:31,571 --> 00:17:32,701
after Bloody Ridge,

453
00:17:32,721 --> 00:17:35,499
the division sent in just one regiment.

454
00:17:35,608 --> 00:17:38,866
The 38th Infantry, held in reserve,

455
00:17:38,886 --> 00:17:42,359
was barely used until Operation Touchdown.

456
00:17:42,483 --> 00:17:44,614
It was the same mistake made at Bloody

457
00:17:44,634 --> 00:17:47,424
Ridge, repeated all over again.

458
00:17:47,627 --> 00:17:49,503
Second: underground bunkers

459
00:17:49,523 --> 00:17:51,514
and unseen mortars.

460
00:17:51,705 --> 00:17:53,625
The shelling stripped away the forest,

461
00:17:53,645 --> 00:17:57,095
but the bunkers under logs and earth survived.

462
00:17:57,153 --> 00:17:59,466
When the barrage lifted, the machine

463
00:17:59,486 --> 00:18:01,185
guns in the bunkers and the mortars

464
00:18:01,205 --> 00:18:03,723
beyond the crest came back to life.

465
00:18:03,791 --> 00:18:06,150
The question was never how many shells,

466
00:18:06,170 --> 00:18:09,057
but how to root out an enemy you could not see.

467
00:18:09,241 --> 00:18:13,013
Third: attacks that hammered a single spot.

468
00:18:13,191 --> 00:18:15,049
Strike the same peak from the same

469
00:18:15,069 --> 00:18:17,428
direction again and again, and the enemy

470
00:18:17,448 --> 00:18:20,216
only had to mass his fire and men there.

471
00:18:20,381 --> 00:18:22,581
Strike from many directions at once,

472
00:18:22,601 --> 00:18:25,129
and he no longer knew where to defend.

473
00:18:25,284 --> 00:18:27,234
Even the U.S. Army's official history

474
00:18:27,254 --> 00:18:30,125
concludes that success came only after the

475
00:18:30,145 --> 00:18:32,365
attack was spread across many points.

476
00:18:32,555 --> 00:18:36,506
Fourth, and decisive: a road for the tanks.

477
00:18:36,678 --> 00:18:38,730
One valley road, cut with shovels

478
00:18:38,750 --> 00:18:40,426
and explosives, carried the

479
00:18:40,446 --> 00:18:42,564
tanks deep behind the ridge.

480
00:18:42,734 --> 00:18:45,240
Once the supply lines were cut, even an

481
00:18:45,260 --> 00:18:47,812
underground fortress could not hold out.

482
00:18:47,907 --> 00:18:49,675
If the lesson of Bloody Ridge

483
00:18:49,695 --> 00:18:51,553
was the flank, the lesson of

484
00:18:51,573 --> 00:18:53,869
Heartbreak Ridge was the road.

485
00:18:54,062 --> 00:18:55,768
With Heartbreak Ridge taken,

486
00:18:55,788 --> 00:18:57,795
X Corps advanced more than eight

487
00:18:57,815 --> 00:19:00,321
kilometers and straightened its line.

488
00:19:00,478 --> 00:19:01,949
The bulge in the front around the

489
00:19:01,969 --> 00:19:04,210
Punchbowl was finally evened out.

490
00:19:04,386 --> 00:19:07,195
On October 25th, the stalled armistice

491
00:19:07,215 --> 00:19:10,176
talks reopened, at Panmunjom.

492
00:19:10,214 --> 00:19:12,084
With its lines being pushed back by

493
00:19:12,104 --> 00:19:14,052
the fall offensive, the Communist

494
00:19:14,072 --> 00:19:15,998
side had returned to the table.

495
00:19:16,048 --> 00:19:19,101
And in November, both sides tentatively

496
00:19:19,121 --> 00:19:21,412
agreed to use the line of contact as

497
00:19:21,432 --> 00:19:23,693
the basis for the demarcation line.

498
00:19:23,887 --> 00:19:25,864
The war dragged on for another one

499
00:19:25,884 --> 00:19:27,809
year and eight months after that.

500
00:19:27,917 --> 00:19:29,707
But the front never strayed far from

501
00:19:29,727 --> 00:19:31,895
the line drawn around this time.

502
00:19:32,095 --> 00:19:32,938
In France, too,

503
00:19:32,958 --> 00:19:34,850
this battle was not forgotten.

504
00:19:34,956 --> 00:19:37,405
In 1955, a French documentary

505
00:19:37,425 --> 00:19:39,892
about it, 'Crèvecœur,' was even

506
00:19:39,912 --> 00:19:42,304
nominated for an Academy Award.

507
00:19:42,413 --> 00:19:44,732
Crèvecœur. In French,

508
00:19:44,752 --> 00:19:47,687
it too means heartbreak.

509
00:19:47,844 --> 00:19:49,031
Some say this fight,

510
00:19:49,051 --> 00:19:51,728
where thousands fell for a few hills,

511
00:19:51,748 --> 00:19:54,050
was a pointless war of attrition.

512
00:19:54,148 --> 00:19:56,575
And indeed, after this, the UN

513
00:19:56,595 --> 00:19:59,284
command turned away from big offensives

514
00:19:59,304 --> 00:20:01,581
toward a largely defensive war.

515
00:20:01,766 --> 00:20:05,107
But today's Demilitarized Zone runs along

516
00:20:05,127 --> 00:20:08,089
the line drawn across those very hills.

517
00:20:08,257 --> 00:20:10,696
Even now, the DMZ fences follow the

518
00:20:10,716 --> 00:20:12,892
mountain ridges north of Yanggu.

519
00:20:12,988 --> 00:20:14,481
Perhaps that is why the Korean

520
00:20:14,501 --> 00:20:17,327
film 'Tae Guk Gi' set its final

521
00:20:17,347 --> 00:20:19,278
battle on this very ridge.

522
00:20:19,615 --> 00:20:20,790
A ridge half a million

523
00:20:20,810 --> 00:20:22,577
shells could not break,

524
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:24,921
was opened in the end by a shovel,

525
00:20:24,941 --> 00:20:26,321
and the wisdom to strike

526
00:20:26,341 --> 00:20:28,332
from many directions at once.

527
00:20:28,528 --> 00:20:30,089
And the price of that wisdom was

528
00:20:30,109 --> 00:20:31,718
paid first by the men who

529
00:20:31,738 --> 00:20:34,329
endured thirty heartbreaking days.

530
00:20:34,530 --> 00:20:36,211
If this story meant something to you,

531
00:20:36,231 --> 00:20:38,418
please subscribe and hit like.

532
00:20:38,489 --> 00:20:39,721
We'll keep telling the forgotten

533
00:20:39,741 --> 00:20:41,869
stories of war in the next video.
